          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">🔄 1. The Reciprocal Nature of Human-Environment Interaction</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  • <strong>Reciprocal Relationship:</strong> People depend on the natural environment for vital survival needs (potable water, fertile soil, food, timber, mineral fuels, living space). In turn, human activities continuously alter and shape the physical environment.<br>\n                  • <strong>Human Impacts:</strong> Deforestation for farmland and fuelwood, atmospheric/water pollution, wetland drainage, urban sprawl, strip mining, and terracing steep hillsides.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400\">🌱 2. Sustainable Resource Use</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  • <strong>Textbook Definition:</strong> <strong>Sustainable use</strong> means utilizing natural resources to meet the needs of the present generation while protecting and preserving resource availability and environmental quality for <strong>future generations</strong>.<br>\n                  • <strong>Core Principles:</strong> Renewable resources must not be consumed faster than their natural regeneration rate; non-renewable resources must be used efficiently with active recycling and transition to clean alternatives.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
