          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">⚖️ 1. Population-Resource Classifications</h4>\n                <div class=\"space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300\">\n                  <p>• <strong>Overpopulation:</strong> A condition where the population size is too large relative to the available resources, technology, and capital, creating severe pressure on food supplies, housing, clean water, and social infrastructure.<br>\n                  • <strong>Underpopulation:</strong> A situation where a country has too few people to exploit, manage, and utilize its abundant natural resources effectively to maximize living standards (e.g. parts of Canada, Australia).<br>\n                  • <strong>Optimal Population:</strong> The ideal population size that produces the highest possible standard of living and per capita income given available resources and existing technology.</p>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400\">🔬 2. Beyond Numbers: Technology and Management</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  Population pressure is not determined solely by headcounts. It depends critically on:\n                  • <strong>Consumption patterns:</strong> Resource usage per capita.<br>\n                  • <strong>Technology:</strong> Efficient farming (irrigation, high-yield seeds), renewable energy, and waste recycling expand carrying capacity.<br>\n                  • <strong>Resource Governance:</strong> Fair distribution and transparent conservation prevent artificial scarcity.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
