          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">📦 1. Definition and Classifications</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  • <strong>Natural Resource:</strong> Any useful material, organism, or environmental condition provided by nature that human beings utilize to satisfy their biological, physical, and economic needs.<br>\n                  • <strong>Renewable Resources:</strong> Resources that can be replenished or regenerated naturally over a relatively short period of time when managed responsibly (e.g. solar energy, wind, flowing water, forests, wildlife, fertile soil).<br>\n                  • <strong>Non-renewable Resources:</strong> Resources that exist in finite quantities and cannot be replaced once depleted because their geological formation takes millions of years (e.g. petroleum, coal, natural gas, iron ore, gold, copper).\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400\">🌿 2. Biotic vs. Abiotic Resources</h4>\n                <div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs text-slate-300\">\n                  <div class=\"p-3 bg-space-950 rounded-lg border border-slate-850\">\n                    <strong class=\"text-emerald-300\">Biotic Resources:</strong><br>\n                    Derived from living organisms and organic matter.<br>\n                    Examples: Forests, crops, livestock, fish, wildlife, fossil fuels (ancient decomposed organisms).\n                  </div>\n                  <div class=\"p-3 bg-space-950 rounded-lg border border-slate-850\">\n                    <strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Abiotic Resources:</strong><br>\n                    Originate from non-living physical substances and conditions.<br>\n                    Examples: Sunlight, fresh water, atmospheric air, rocks, metallic ores, sand, soil minerals.\n                  </div>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
