          "coreNotes": "\n            <div class=\"space-y-4 text-slate-200\">\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-amber-400\">🌾 1. Agriculture and Pastoralism</h4>\n                <p class=\"text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed\">\n                  • <strong>Highland Agriculture:</strong> The bedrock of medieval Ethiopian states was ox-drawn plow agriculture (Maresha). Highland farmers cultivated teff, barley, wheat, sorghum, and enset.<br>\n                  • <strong>Pastoralism:</strong> In the arid lowlands, pastoralist communities raised cattle, camels, sheep, and goats, trading animal products with highland farming communities for grain.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <div class=\"p-4 rounded-xl bg-space-900 border border-slate-800 space-y-2\">\n                <h4 class=\"text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400\">🐪 2. Trade Networks and State Taxation</h4>\n                <div class=\"space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300\">\n                  <p>• <strong>Caravan Corridors:</strong> Long-distance trade routes linked the southwestern interior (Kaffa, Jimma, Ennarea) with the Red Sea (Massawa) and Gulf of Aden (Zeila). Merchants moved in protected armed caravans using pack animals (mules, donkeys, camels).<br>\n                  • <strong>Mediums of Exchange:</strong> Barter, iron bars, pieces of cloth, glass beads, and most notably <strong>Amole</strong> (standardized blocks of rock salt quarried from the Danakil Depression).<br>\n                  • <strong>State Taxation (Gult / Tribute):</strong> Monarchs and regional rulers levied tribute on agricultural production and collected customs toll taxes (Kella) at fortified checkpoints along trade routes to finance armies, court administrations, and monumental church/palace construction.</p>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          ",
